[Music]  so  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii fortune of video archive testimonies  hold a wide  variety of songs and poetry  the pre-war pieces alone formed quite a  large group  they include traditional songs of many  genres  military songs but honest the wedding  gestures cooplays and recitatives  zionist songs bundest songs communist  songs  really a wide variety of music and  poetry  jack m a native of the polish town of  schwarvetz  is remembering the songs that he heard  and remembered from the pre-war period  they're not only important because they  constitute a large  part of the ethnographic research of  polish jewry of polish culture of jewish  culture  but they also portray uh how the  people were really formed and shaped  uh before the war  and uh they help us understand  who those people really were as normal  human beings  not as just inmates as prisoners  at concentration camps and lay slave  labor camps  but human beings polish citizens  jews in his testimony jack  is remembering uh these  songs that he heard as he was serving in  the polish army  and those are quite interesting ones  uh the two songs that he remembered uh  were coming mostly from his commander in  his regiment  and one is quite openly anti-semitic  and the other one is rather frivolous  jack remembered them he did not consider  them  really negative but that  probably tells us about the degree of  tolerance  that the targeted group of population  groups societal group develops as a  survival tool  they were laughing at on us the  the christians anticipating all the  ground  in the the the made up a song  they took they took a jew to them  to the army in  in instead to give him  rose with bare  he gave him a cabbage soup they made up  a song like that  you remember the song yes in police  they took her to a  the gun should be loaded with ammunition  they put in the onion an onion in and  they asked him he should  he should shoot through his own wife  that was  too too late were there songs that you  knew in the army or sang in the army  from what  i said  yeah their song goes like that  about a guru  nevada soldier think that you  you understand  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  this is a merry song here  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  i'm talking today about um  two songs which were recorded by an  amateur folk singer  a jack millstein a polish jew  he served in the polish army he was born  in 1913 so he served  in the polish army in the late uh  20s and early 30s  this was a difficult time i think for  jews in the polish army  the ncos the officers indeed the men  were not that sympathetic towards jewish  recruits  particularly ones who were not that  acculturated uh  milstein speaks sings his two songs  in polish i mean he has a slight accent  in polish but  his polish is clearly good and he has no  problems either  recording in polish or indeed presumably  speaking in polish  the first song is a song  so-called humorous song mocking jewish  recruits  instead of loading his gun with bullets  the jewish recruit loads his gun with  onions  because onions and jews like garlic and  jews is one of those  east european tropes not only does he  load his gun with  onions but he shoots at his wife  and his wife is called not kobeta but  kobita the woman  it's a rather lower class  a polish expression the other song he  sings  is a love song and it's a rather  attractive song  i mean i don't see it as being humorous  or in any sense  anti-jewish and what it reflects in my  view is the degree to which  polish popular culture had penetrated  into  the jewish world in the 1920s and the  1930s  although particularly after 1935  anti-semitism increased in poland and  indeed  in the early years of the pilsudski  regime after persuski to power in  may 1926 the government  attempted to reach an understanding with  the various national minorities  and indeed with the jews and until the  death of general pelsicki in may 1935  jews felt relatively comfortable but  throughout this period  in the period when the jewish situation  was relatively  good after 1926 and as it deteriorated  first under the impact of the great  depression and then after the death of  marshall kasich when a section of the  government camp  attempted to strengthen its position by  making  common cause with the anti-semitic part  of the polish right  there was a high degree of acculturation  taking place  so along with the deterioration of the  situation of the jews  they went in adoption of the polish  language and i think  this is reflected in milstein's  songs he sings songs in polish he has no  problem singing these songs in polish  and  he's clearly quite comfortable singing  them in polish  so they reflect on the one hand the  ambivalent position of the jews  relatively stable in the late 1920s and  early 1930s when milstein is serving in  the  polish army deteriorating after 1935  but also it reflects the growing  acculturation of jews  and one element in this which perhaps is  linked with these  songs is the fact that polish popular  culture  polish popular music polish songs  were an area where jews  were extremely active jews were among  the people who produced chairs  the vlasts and the other  major jewish singers these were people  who were  accepted on the larger polish scene and  as in the united states  the polish equivalent of tin pan alley  had a heavily  jewish component  [Music]  you

